WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:03.570
- [Narrator] Taylor Swift, the
singer with a big reputation

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:06.150
in the music industry,
is according to Forbes,

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:10.080
worth an estimated $570 million.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:13.560
And with Eras forecast to
become the highest grossing tour

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:14.400
of all time,

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:17.178
the singer could considerably
add to her net worth.

00:00:17.178 --> 00:00:18.720
(crowd cheering)

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:20.431
So how did she get here?

00:00:20.431 --> 00:00:21.630
(crowd screaming)

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:23.580
- 53% of the adults

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:26.250
in the United States considered themselves

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:27.810
to be Taylor Swift fans

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:32.040
and 16% considered themselves
to be avid Swifties.

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:35.220
- [Narrator] For Swift, that
translates into record sales,

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:37.710
record-breaking record sales.

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:39.780
Her 10th studio album, "Midnights,"

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:41.850
released in October 2022,

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:45.180
sold 1.6 million albums in its first week

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:46.830
according to Universal Music.

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:47.790
That makes her the one

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:50.610
and only artist to ever
have five albums sell

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:53.490
over one million copies in its first week.

00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:56.790
The resurgence of vinyl
records has also played a role.

00:00:56.790 --> 00:01:01.790
In 2022, vinyls outsold CDs
for the first time since 1987.

00:01:01.830 --> 00:01:06.090
Taylor's album "Midnights"
sold 575,000 vinyls

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:07.380
in its first week.

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:12.380
- A typical stream generates
well under a penny in revenue

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:18.300
from the streaming service
that the stream originates on.

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:23.300
Somewhere between 1/4 and 3/4
of a penny for all royaltors

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:26.850
and all participants
in that income stream.

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:29.820
When you sell a vinyl record today,

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:33.270
which at retail costs 20 to $30,

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:36.720
well, you can imagine that
the numbers are much better

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:40.800
on a per unit basis,
certainly than a stream.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:43.560
- [Narrator] Still, streaming
has significantly contributed

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:44.640
to Taylor's wealth.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:45.870
According to Universal,

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:50.430
the singer has 36.6 billion
combined streams of her music

00:01:50.430 --> 00:01:52.500
but there was a time
when streaming services

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:54.870
and Taylor were on rocky ground.

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:59.400
From 2014 to 2017, Swift
boycotted Spotify removing all

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:01.080
of her music from the platform.

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:03.450
In 2015, Swift wrote an open letter

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:05.790
to Apple objecting to
the company's decision

00:02:05.790 --> 00:02:07.950
to not pay artists streaming royalties

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:10.500
during the three-month
Apple Music trial period.

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:13.440
- Apple to their credit, came
back and said, you're right.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:15.810
We've changed our mind about this

00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:19.710
and we will pay artists
for the use of music

00:02:19.710 --> 00:02:21.210
during the trial period.

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:23.070
- [Narrator] When her
album "Midnights" released,

00:02:23.070 --> 00:02:25.470
it became Spotify's most streamed album

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:27.720
in a single day and also broke the record

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:30.960
for the most streamed artist
in a single day on Spotify.

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:32.100
Money from music streams

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:34.860
and sales doesn't just go to the artist.

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:37.350
In 2022, Swift generated close

00:02:37.350 --> 00:02:42.300
to 3% of Universal Music's
annual revenue, or $230 million

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:44.790
in sales for the biggest
music label in the world

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:46.560
according to JPMorgan.

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:48.150
- In every piece of music that you hear,

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:50.850
there are two copyrights involved.

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:54.000
The copyright in the
underlying musical composition,

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:55.140
the song itself.

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:58.500
That's where songwriters
and music publishers live.

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:02.190
Once the song is made
into a sound recording,

00:03:02.190 --> 00:03:04.410
that's usually where the record company

00:03:04.410 --> 00:03:05.790
and the performer live.

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:08.070
- [Narrator] Typically,
record labels own the masters

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:10.290
of a song or the original recording.

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:13.320
That means the labels keep the
fee for master recording use

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:16.080
every time a song is
streamed, performed on TV

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:17.430
or played in a movie.

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:20.670
In 2019, Scooter Braun
managing Ithaca Holdings

00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:22.350
bought Big Machine Label Group,

00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:24.450
which owned the masters to her recordings.

00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:26.670
He then spun off her masters.

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:28.590
- After the catalog sale had happened,

00:03:28.590 --> 00:03:33.090
Taylor would still be
a royalty participant

00:03:33.090 --> 00:03:36.600
as the artist who made the
records, but the vast majority

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:38.879
of that income would be going to the owner

00:03:38.879 --> 00:03:41.670
of the catalog, Ithaca Holdings.

00:03:41.670 --> 00:03:44.220
- [Narrator] In 2018,
Swift revealed on Instagram

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:46.050
that she signed with Republic Records,

00:03:46.050 --> 00:03:48.330
a label under Universal Music Group.

00:03:48.330 --> 00:03:49.320
As part of the deal,

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:52.470
Swift said she would own
her future master recordings

00:03:52.470 --> 00:03:54.390
and her contract with her previous home,

00:03:54.390 --> 00:03:55.710
Big Machine Label Group,

00:03:55.710 --> 00:03:58.200
allowed her to rerecord her old songs.

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:00.720
- In most traditional artists agreements,

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:04.020
most artists are barred
for a really long period

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:08.580
of time from rerecording
their original works.

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:12.660
Taylor, as her career became
the fantastic juggernaut

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:13.493
that it did,

00:04:13.493 --> 00:04:16.260
gained a tremendous amount
of leverage in the deals

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:19.350
that she was able to make
while still on Big Machine.

00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:23.460
I'm not sure if the provisions
allowed for her to rerecord

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:26.670
within a very short period
of time after a sale or not

00:04:26.670 --> 00:04:30.450
but we do know that the period
was very truncated, much more

00:04:30.450 --> 00:04:34.470
than it normally is for the
conventional artist deal.

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:36.270
- [Narrator] So far, Swift has re-released

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:39.360
her albums "Fearless" and
"Red" as Taylor's version,

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:40.193
both making it

00:04:40.193 --> 00:04:43.320
to the top of Billboard charts
and selling more album units

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:45.870
in the first week than
their original releases.

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:48.480
For any sales of these,
Swift receives the artist

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:50.700
and master's share of the song revenue.

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:52.260
- It was to Taylor's benefit

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:54.450
to be able to maximize her income

00:04:54.450 --> 00:04:57.450
and give the fans, you know, new versions

00:04:57.450 --> 00:05:00.780
of the songs that they
knew and loved so much

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:04.440
and have the income come directly to her

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:06.000
or to the administrators

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:09.180
who are managing the new records for her.

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:12.040
- [Narrator] Then of course,
there are Taylor Swift's tours.

00:05:12.040 --> 00:05:14.850
(upbeat music)

00:05:14.850 --> 00:05:16.440
- When the Taylor tour went on sale,

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:19.590
it broke the internet and it
broke Ticketmaster's system.

00:05:19.590 --> 00:05:21.480
There are very few artists

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:25.500
in the world who face
virtually infinite demand

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:28.710
for seats to their concerts
at almost any price.

00:05:28.710 --> 00:05:33.710
When Ticketmaster put the
Taylor Swift tour on sale,

00:05:34.290 --> 00:05:37.740
essentially there were three
and a half billion requests

00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:39.930
for one and a half million seats.

00:05:39.930 --> 00:05:42.780
- [Narrator] Tours are a
huge money maker for artists.

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:47.780
- 85 or 90% or more of the
face value of the ticket goes

00:05:48.540 --> 00:05:52.350
to pay the artist, but not
just the artist themselves.

00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:55.860
I mean, to put a big tour on
the road is really expensive

00:05:55.860 --> 00:06:00.860
so it pays for the artist and
everyone in the artist's camp

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:04.590
from everybody you see
on stage to the people

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:06.990
who move the equipment
in and out, to the people

00:06:06.990 --> 00:06:09.000
who operate the sound
and lights to the people

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:12.930
who drive the trucks and
fly the planes around

00:06:12.930 --> 00:06:14.910
and negotiate the deals.

00:06:14.910 --> 00:06:18.420
But overwhelmingly, most of
what the ticket price goes to

00:06:18.420 --> 00:06:19.830
is to pay for the artist

00:06:19.830 --> 00:06:20.790
- [Narrator] And the Eras tour

00:06:20.790 --> 00:06:24.150
could gross $728 million according

00:06:24.150 --> 00:06:26.910
to concert data tracker
Pollstar, which also predicts

00:06:26.910 --> 00:06:28.470
that the tour could set the record

00:06:28.470 --> 00:06:31.260
for the highest grossing
tour in a calendar year.

00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:34.800
So what keeps Taylor Swift
fans in love with her music?

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:37.050
- Hundreds of millions of people

00:06:37.050 --> 00:06:40.440
around the world relate to her music

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:45.440
in a powerful and profound
and utterly relatable way.

00:06:46.620 --> 00:06:49.830
We're talking about a music creator

00:06:49.830 --> 00:06:54.090
and performer whose
music changes the lives

00:06:54.090 --> 00:06:57.360
of the people who relate so closely to it.

00:06:57.360 --> 00:07:00.510
That makes Taylor utterly unique

00:07:00.510 --> 00:07:05.510
in the constellation of
music makers, well known

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:11.038
and less well known over the
last 10 years in particular.

00:07:11.038 --> 00:07:13.621
(upbeat music)

